<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Auckland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Auckland is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, on an isthmus between two harbours in the north of the North Island. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-hobson">William Hobson</a> chose the site as the colonial capital in 1840, and it remained the capital until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> replaced it in 1865. It grew into the country's main commercial centre and port and has the largest Polynesian population of any city in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/auckland.jpg" alt="Auckland" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: elpinto007 · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Auckland_skyline_-_May_2024_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Auckland lies in the north of the North Island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, on an isthmus less than two kilometres wide at its narrowest point, between the Manukau Harbour, which opens west to the Tasman Sea, and the Waitematā Harbour, which extends east to the Hauraki Gulf and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>. The city straddles the Auckland Volcanic Field, which has produced at least 53 small volcanic centres over about 193,000 years. The most recent eruption, about 1450 CE at Rangitoto, was witnessed by Māori living in the area. Its Māori name is Tāmaki Makaurau, meaning Tāmaki desired by many.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Māori settled the isthmus around 1350 and valued its rich and fertile land. They built many pā, or fortified villages, mainly on the volcanic peaks. By the early 1700s a confederation called Te Waiohua was the main group, and from the 1740s Ngāti Whātua Ōrākei were the main tribe. The Māori population is estimated to have been about 20,000 before the arrival of Europeans. Firearms, introduced at the end of the eighteenth century, brought devastating intertribal warfare from 1807.</p><p>After a British colony was established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> in 1840, Lieutenant-Governor William Hobson chose Auckland as its capital, on land that Ngāti Whātua Ōrākei had given him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> replaced Auckland as capital in 1865, but Auckland continued to grow, at first through its port and the logging and gold-mining of its hinterland, then through dairy farming nearby and manufacturing in the city. It has been the largest city in the country through most of its history. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the motor vehicle dominated its transport, and suburbs such as the North Shore grew, especially after the Auckland Harbour Bridge was built in the late 1950s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Auckland is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s most populous city and its leading economic hub, and the University of Auckland, founded in 1883, is the largest university in the country. Since the late 20th century the city has become multicultural. It is home to the largest ethnic Polynesian population of any city in the world, including many Pacific Islanders and Māori. Its landmarks include the Harbour Bridge, the Town Hall, the Ferry Building, the Sky Tower and the Auckland War Memorial Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Auckland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Māori moved from rural communities to the cities on a large scale after the Second World War, and from the 1960s migrants from Samoa, the Cook Islands, Tonga and Niue made <b>Auckland</b> the largest Polynesian city in the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Protest against the South African rugby team’s tour of New Zealand in 1981 was so fierce that during the final Test, at Eden Park in <b>Auckland</b>, a light aircraft flew low over the ground dropping flour bombs onto the pitch.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The movement also intended to establish a system of law and order in Māori communities, to which the <b>Auckland</b> government had so far shown little interest.</blockquote><a href="/movement/maori-king-movement">Māori King movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Its video was taken from a television special filmed at the Mandalay nightclub in Newmarket, <b>Auckland</b>, and it was later included in Nature's Best 3, a collection of the top 100 New Zealand songs as voted by members of APRA.</blockquote><a href="/songs/e-ipo">E Ipo</a></li><li><blockquote>The Māori version was made in 1878 by Thomas Henry Smith of <b>Auckland</b>, a judge of the Native Land Court, at the request of Premier George Edward Grey, and printed under the title &quot;Aotearoa&quot; in Otago newspapers in October 1878.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-defend-new-zealand">God Defend New Zealand</a></li><li><blockquote>…Eastern Māori politicians Paraire Tomoana and Āpirana Ngata published it in 1919, but neither claimed to have written it: they explained that it had come from north of <b>Auckland</b> and had been made popular by Māori soldiers training near Auckland before they sailed for the war in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/songs/pokarekare-ana">Pōkarekare Ana</a></li></ul></section>
